# 1. PostgreSQL Architecture & Query Processing

## 1.1 Connection-to-result flow

```text
Application
   │
   │ SQL connection / query
   ▼
PostgreSQL server
   │
   ▼
Connection manager / postmaster
   │
   ├── Check pg_hba.conf
   │      └── Authentication / access allowed?
   │
   ▼
Backend process
   │
   ▼
Parser
   ├── Lexing / syntax check
   └── Parse tree
   ▼
Analyzer / Transformer
   ├── Resolve tables / columns / data types
   └── Query tree
   ▼
Rewriter
   └── Rules / view expansion
   ▼
Planner / Optimizer
   ├── Seq Scan
   ├── Index Scan
   ├── Bitmap Scan
   ├── Nested Loop
   ├── Hash Join
   └── Merge Join
   │
   └── Choose lowest estimated cost
   ▼
Executor
   ├── Shared buffers / storage
   └── Return rows
   ▼
Application
```

> ⭐ Connection → pg_hba.conf → backend → parse → analyze → rewrite → plan → execute → result

## 1.2 Important architecture components

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Postmaster / main server process | Manages connections and background processes. |
| Backend process | Handles a client session and query. |
| Shared buffers | PostgreSQL's main shared cache for data pages. |
| WAL buffers | Memory area where WAL records are held before writing to WAL files. |
| Heap | Main table storage. |
| Index | Separate access structure used to find rows efficiently. |
| TOAST | Stores oversized values outside normal row storage. |
| Background processes | Handles WAL writing, checkpoints, autovacuum, stats, maintenance. |

## 1.3 pg_hba.conf vs listen_addresses

| Parameter / file | What it answers |
|---|---|
| listen_addresses | Which server IP interfaces should PostgreSQL listen on? |
| pg_hba.conf | Which client/database/user/address is allowed and how is it authenticated? |

`listen_addresses` accepts IP addresses or `*`; CIDR ranges belong in the address field of `pg_hba.conf`.

