# 2. Security, Users, Roles & Privileges

## 2.1 Three security layers

| Layer | Question | Main control |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Connection | Can you connect at all? | pg_hba.conf + listen_addresses |
| 2. Database / schema | What can you use after connecting? | CONNECT, CREATE, USAGE |
| 3. Object | What can you do to the data? | SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE |

## 2.2 Common pg_hba.conf methods

| Method | Meaning |
|---|---|
| trust | Allows connection without password; dangerous in production. |
| password | Password authentication; use securely, preferably with SSL. |
| md5 | Legacy password authentication. |
| scram-sha-256 | Modern password authentication; preferred for new setups. |

```sql
SELECT pg_reload_conf();

REVOKE CONNECT, CREATE ON DATABASE pdb FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE pdb TO app_user;

GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO app_user;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON employee TO app_user;
```

## 2.3 Users, roles and groups

Since PostgreSQL 8.1, users, roles and groups are represented by the same underlying role system. The practical difference is mostly default attributes.

| Command | Default behavior |
|---|---|
| CREATE USER | Creates a role with LOGIN by default. |
| CREATE ROLE | Creates a role with NOLOGIN by default. |
| CREATE GROUP | Same underlying role concept; NOLOGIN by default. |

## 2.4 Important role attributes

| Attribute | Meaning |
|---|---|
| LOGIN | Role can connect to PostgreSQL. |
| SUPERUSER | Bypasses normal permission checks. |
| CREATEDB | Can create databases. |
| CREATEROLE | Can create/manage roles. |
| REPLICATION | Can perform replication-related connections/operations. |
| PASSWORD | Authentication credential for password-based login. |

## 2.5 Predefined roles to remember

| Role | Interview meaning |
|---|---|
| pg_database_owner | Dynamic role representing the current database owner. |
| pg_read_all_stats | Can view statistics/activity for other users. |
| pg_read_all_settings | Can view configuration settings, including normally restricted ones. |
| pg_read_all_data | Read-only access to all tables/views/sequences and schema usage; still needs CONNECT. |
| pg_write_all_data | Write access counterpart to pg_read_all_data. |
| pg_monitor | Bundle of monitoring-related predefined roles. |
| pg_signal_backend | Allows permitted cancellation/termination of other non-superuser backends. |
| pg_checkpoint | Allows a non-superuser to run CHECKPOINT. |
| pg_maintain | Maintenance operations such as VACUUM, ANALYZE, CLUSTER, REINDEX without table ownership; added in PG17. |
| pg_read_server_files | Read server-side files. |
| pg_write_server_files | Write/export to server-side files. |
| pg_execute_server_program | Run server-side programs through supported operations such as COPY PROGRAM. |
| pg_use_reserved_connections | Use reserved connection slots when normal capacity is exhausted. |

## 2.6 Useful security / monitoring views

```sql
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_activity;
SELECT * FROM pg_settings;
SELECT * FROM pg_db_role_settings;
SELECT * FROM pg_shadow;   -- superuser-restricted view; pg_authid is the underlying source
```

> ⭐ `pg_hba.conf` = can I connect? Privileges = what can I do after connecting?

