# 4. Memory & Important Performance Parameters

| Parameter | Purpose | Remember |
|---|---|---|
| shared_buffers | PostgreSQL's internal cache. | Actual memory allocation. |
| work_mem | Memory per operation such as sort/hash; can be used multiple times per query and across sessions. | Dynamic; do not multiply by max_connections blindly. |
| maintenance_work_mem | Memory for maintenance tasks such as VACUUM and index creation. | Maintenance, not normal sorting. |
| autovacuum_work_mem | Memory used by autovacuum workers. | Per autovacuum worker. |
| effective_cache_size | Planner estimate of cache available from PostgreSQL + OS cache. | Does NOT allocate memory. |
| temp_buffers | Memory for temporary tables per session. | Session-specific. |
| huge_pages | Uses larger memory pages when supported by OS. | Reduces page-management overhead. |
| max_worker_processes | Cluster-wide ceiling for background/parallel workers. | Upper ceiling. |
| max_parallel_workers | Parallel worker ceiling. | Must fit under max_worker_processes. |
| max_parallel_workers_per_gather | Max parallel workers for one Gather/Gather Merge. | One query's parallelism ceiling. |

> ⭐ `effective_cache_size` is a planner estimate, not allocated RAM. Higher values can make index plans look cheaper; lower values can make sequential scans more attractive.

## 4.1 Parallel worker hierarchy

```text
max_worker_processes ≥ max_parallel_workers ≥ max_parallel_workers_per_gather
```

- Each parallel worker is a separate process with its own CPU and memory usage.
- The optimizer decides whether parallelism is worthwhile.
- Test session-level changes before making cluster-wide changes.

