# 5. Query Performance & EXPLAIN

## 5.1 EXPLAIN vs EXPLAIN ANALYZE

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| EXPLAIN | Planner estimates the execution plan; query is not executed. |
| EXPLAIN ANALYZE | Executes the query and reports actual rows/time/loops in addition to estimates. |
| EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) | Adds buffer activity such as shared hit/read/dirtied/written. |

Important: `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on `INSERT` / `UPDATE` / `DELETE` really executes the statement. For safe testing, use a transaction and `ROLLBACK`.

```sql
BEGIN;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) UPDATE employee SET salary = 50000 WHERE employee_id = 10;
ROLLBACK;
```

## 5.2 Buffer terminology

| Metric | Meaning |
|---|---|
| shared hit | Page already found in shared_buffers. |
| shared read | Page had to be read from storage/OS cache into shared_buffers. |
| shared dirtied | Page was modified in shared_buffers. |
| shared written | Dirty page was written out during the query. |

## 5.3 Estimated vs actual rows

`ANALYZE` updates planner statistics. If estimated rows and actual rows differ greatly, stale or unrepresentative statistics may be causing a poor plan.

```sql
ANALYZE employee;
VACUUM (ANALYZE) employee;
```

> ⭐ Estimated rows = what the planner expected. Actual rows = what execution really produced. A large mismatch is a strong troubleshooting clue.

## 5.4 Planner cost parameters

| Parameter | Meaning |
|---|---|
| random_page_cost | Estimated cost of random page access relative to sequential access. |
| seq_page_cost | Estimated cost of sequential page access. |
| cpu_tuple_cost | CPU cost for processing a row. |
| cpu_index_tuple_cost | CPU cost for processing an index entry. |
| effective_cache_size | Planner estimate of cache availability. |

## 5.5 Work_mem and disk spill

Sorts and hash operations can use `work_mem`. If an operation needs more memory than available for that operation, it may spill to temporary files.

> ⭐ High temp-file usage or disk-based sorts/hashes can indicate insufficient `work_mem` for that workload; but increasing it globally can also increase memory pressure.

