# 6. Scan Types & Join Methods

## 6.1 Sequential Scan vs Index Scan vs Bitmap Scan

| Method | Simple meaning | Good fit |
|---|---|---|
| Seq Scan | Reads the table sequentially. | Large portion of table must be read; small tables; planner cost favors it. |
| Index Scan | Uses an index to find matching entries, then fetches heap rows. | Selective lookups. |
| Bitmap Index Scan + Bitmap Heap Scan | Builds a bitmap of matching heap locations, then fetches pages efficiently. | Many matching rows where individual random index lookups would be expensive. |

> ⭐ B-tree = index structure. Bitmap Index Scan = execution method that can use an index to find many matching rows.

## 6.2 Join methods

| Join | How it works | Good fit / tuning clue |
|---|---|---|
| Nested Loop | For each outer row, find matching inner rows. | Small outer input + indexed inner join column. |
| Hash Join | Build a hash table from one input, then probe it with the other. | Equality joins; enough work_mem reduces batching. |
| Merge Join | Processes two sorted inputs together. | Large inputs where sorted or indexed inputs are useful. |

## 6.3 Hash join memory

The hash table uses `work_mem` multiplied by `hash_mem_multiplier`. If the hash table does not fit, PostgreSQL may use multiple batches, which can increase execution time.

```sql
SET enable_hashjoin = off;
SET enable_mergejoin = off;
SET enable_nestloop = off;
```

These settings are useful for testing and comparison, not as a normal tuning solution.

