# 7. PostgreSQL Indexes

| Index | Best fit | Memory line |
|---|---|---|
| B-tree | Equality, range, ordering, BETWEEN, IN, IS NULL. | Default general-purpose index. |
| Hash | Equality only. | Narrow use case; no ordering/range. |
| GiST | Ranges, overlap, containment, geometric/nearest-neighbor workloads. | Flexible generalized search tree. |
| SP-GiST | Naturally partitionable/non-balanced data such as prefixes or IP structures. | Space-partitioned access method. |
| GIN | Arrays, JSONB containment, full-text style multi-value searches. | Value → matching rows. |
| BRIN | Huge naturally ordered tables such as time-series data. | Very small summary index; relies on physical correlation. |

## 7.1 Useful index commands

```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_emp_salary ON employees(salary);
CREATE INDEX idx_name ON table_name USING btree (column_name);
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_name ON table_name (column_name);

SELECT indexname, indexdef
FROM pg_indexes
WHERE tablename = 'table_name';

SELECT *
FROM pg_stat_user_indexes
WHERE relname = 'table_name';

SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('idx_name'));

REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_name;
```

## 7.2 Index strategy

- Start with the query pattern, not simply the data type.
- `WHERE`, `JOIN`, and `ORDER BY` are common index candidates.
- Avoid unnecessary indexes because they add storage and write/maintenance overhead.
- An index existing does not guarantee the planner will use it.
- Use `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` to verify actual behavior.

