# 8. VACUUM, ANALYZE, AUTOVACUUM & Bloat

| Operation | Purpose | Lock / space behavior |
|---|---|---|
| VACUUM | Removes dead tuples and makes space reusable inside the table; updates FSM/VM. | Allows normal reads/writes; does not normally return file space to OS. |
| VACUUM FULL | Rewrites the table and returns unused space to OS. | ACCESS EXCLUSIVE; blocks normal activity on that table. |
| ANALYZE | Refreshes planner statistics. | Statistics maintenance. |
| pg_repack | Online table/index reorganization with a short final lock. | Useful when long blocking from VACUUM FULL is unacceptable. |

## 8.1 Autovacuum trigger formula

```sql
VACUUM trigger =
autovacuum_vacuum_threshold
+
(autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor × estimated table rows)
```

Example: threshold 50 + (0.2 × 10,000 rows) = about 2,050 dead tuples.

| Parameter | Purpose |
|---|---|
| autovacuum | Enable automatic vacuum/analyze. |
| autovacuum_max_workers | Number of autovacuum workers. |
| autovacuum_naptime | Delay between autovacuum launcher checks. |
| autovacuum_vacuum_threshold | Fixed dead-tuple threshold. |
| autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor | Fraction of table size added to threshold. |
| autovacuum_analyze_threshold | Fixed threshold for analyze. |
| autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor | Fraction used for analyze trigger. |
| autovacuum_freeze_max_age | Threshold related to transaction ID freezing. |

## 8.2 Why autovacuum may not clean dead tuples

- Long-running transactions can keep old row versions visible.
- Hot standby feedback can prevent cleanup needed by standby queries.
- Replication slots can retain WAL and create pressure.
- Autovacuum settings may be too conservative.
- Autovacuum may be disabled or unable to keep up.
- Investigate `pg_stat_activity`, `pg_stat_user_tables`, `pg_stat_progress_vacuum`, `pg_replication_slots`, and related settings.

