# 9. Transactions, MVCC, Locks & XID Wraparound

## 9.1 MVCC

PostgreSQL uses Multi-Version Concurrency Control. `UPDATE` creates a new row version instead of overwriting the old version. Readers use transaction snapshots to decide which version they can see.

| System column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| xmin | Transaction ID that created the row version. |
| xmax | Transaction ID associated with deleting/updating the row version. |
| ctid | Physical tuple location; it can change when a row is updated. |

> ⭐ MVCC allows readers and writers to operate concurrently by keeping multiple row versions.

## 9.2 XID wraparound

Transaction IDs are 32-bit and wrap around. PostgreSQL freezes old tuples so very old transaction IDs are not confused with new ones after wraparound.

```sql
SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid)
FROM pg_database;

SELECT relname, age(relfrozenxid)
FROM pg_class;
```

> ⭐ VACUUM / AUTOVACUUM + freezing protects PostgreSQL from transaction ID wraparound.

## 9.3 Locks and deadlocks

| Concept | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Row-level lock | Protects affected rows from conflicting operations. |
| Table-level lock | Controls access to table-level operations. |
| Blocked session | Waiting for another session's lock/resource. |
| Blocking session | Holding the resource needed by another session. |
| Deadlock | Two or more transactions wait for each other in a cycle. |

Simple example:

Imagine Session 1 updates an employee's salary but has not committed the transaction.

- Session 1 — Blocking session: holds a lock on the employee row.
- Session 2 — Blocked session: tries to update the same row and must wait.

```sql
SELECT pid, xact_start, wait_event, wait_event_type,
       pg_blocking_pids(pid)
FROM pg_stat_activity;
```

Before terminating a session, identify what it is doing.
Use `pg_cancel_backend()` for a softer query cancellation. Use `pg_terminate_backend()` when the whole backend must be terminated and it is operationally safe.

