# 10. Storage Internals

## 10.1 Data directory — important folders

| Directory | Purpose |
|---|---|
| base/ | Per-database relation files. |
| global/ | Cluster-wide system catalogs. |
| pg_tblspc/ | Links to user-defined tablespace locations. |
| pg_xact/ | Transaction commit/abort status. |
| pg_commit_ts/ | Commit timestamps when enabled. |
| pg_twophase/ | Prepared two-phase transaction state. |
| pg_subtrans/ | Subtransaction → parent transaction relationships. |
| pg_replslot/ | Physical and logical replication slot state. |
| pg_logical/ | Logical decoding working data. |
| pg_dynshmem/ | Dynamic shared-memory files. |
| pg_stat/ / pg_stat_tmp/ | Statistics-related storage; newer versions use shared memory heavily. |

## 10.2 Relation files

Tables and indexes have relation files identified by `relfilenode`. Large relations are split into segment files.

```sql
SELECT pg_relation_filepath('table_name');
```

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| main relation file | Main table/index data. |
| _fsm | Free Space Map — tracks free space in pages. |
| _vm | Visibility Map — tracks pages marked all-visible and all-frozen where applicable. |

## 10.3 Visibility Map — simple explanation

```text
Heap page 1 → all-visible ✓
Heap page 2 → not all-visible ✗
Heap page 3 → all-visible ✓
```

All-visible means PostgreSQL knows that tuples on that heap page are visible to all relevant transactions, so an `Index-Only Scan` can often avoid visiting the heap just to perform visibility checks.

> ⭐ VM = visibility. FSM = free space.

