# 11. WAL, Checkpoints & Background Writing

## 11.1 WAL

Write-Ahead Logging means required WAL records are written before the corresponding data pages are considered durable. WAL is central to crash recovery, replication, and PITR.

| Item | Purpose |
|---|---|
| pg_wal/ | Stores WAL segment files. |
| pg_waldump | Reads and decodes WAL records for investigation. |
| pg_stat_wal | WAL generation and flush statistics. |
| pg_switch_wal() | Forces a WAL segment switch. |
| min_wal_size | Minimum WAL disk space PostgreSQL tries to keep available for reuse. |
| max_wal_size | Soft WAL size target that influences when checkpoints are triggered. |

## 11.2 Checkpoints

| Checkpoint trigger | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Time-based | `checkpoint_timeout` interval reached. |
| Size-based | WAL volume reaches `max_wal_size`. |
| Manual | `CHECKPOINT` command. |

`checkpoint_completion_target` spreads checkpoint writes across the checkpoint interval to smooth I/O.

> ⭐ Prefer a workload where checkpoints are mostly timed rather than constantly requested because `max_wal_size` is being reached too quickly.

## 11.3 Background writer / checkpointer / WAL writer

| Component | High-level responsibility |
|---|---|
| WAL writer | Writes WAL records from WAL buffers toward WAL files. |
| Checkpointer | Writes dirty data pages as part of checkpoints. |
| Background writer | Writes dirty buffers in the background to keep clean buffers available. |

## 11.4 pg_stat_bgwriter clues

| Metric | Interpretation |
|---|---|
| checkpoints_timed | Timed checkpoints. |
| checkpoints_req | Requested/forced checkpoints; high values can indicate WAL pressure. |
| buffers_checkpoint | Buffers written during checkpoints. |
| buffers_clean | Buffers written by background writer. |
| buffers_backend | Buffers written directly by client backends; high values can indicate pressure. |

