# 12. Physical Streaming Replication

## 12.1 Architecture

```text
PRIMARY
  │
  │ WAL
  ▼
WAL Sender
  │
  │ streaming
  ▼
WAL Receiver
  │
  ▼
Standby WAL
  │
  ▼
WAL Replay
  │
  ▼
Standby database
```

> ⭐ Physical streaming replication = WAL-based replication from primary to standby.

## 12.2 LSNs

| LSN | Meaning |
|---|---|
| sent_lsn | WAL sent by primary up to this point. |
| write_lsn | WAL received and written to standby WAL files. |
| flush_lsn | WAL flushed/synced to durable storage on standby. |
| replay_lsn | WAL actually replayed/applied on standby. |

A gap between `sent` and `write` points toward network or receiving delay; `write` to `flush` points toward standby storage latency; `flush` to `replay` points toward replay/CPU/read-workload pressure.

```sql
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_replication;
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_wal_receiver;
SELECT pg_is_in_recovery();
```

## 12.3 Basic physical replication setup

```text
wal_level = replica
archive_mode = on
wal_keep_size = 100MB        # example from notes; choose based on workload
wal_log_hints = on            # useful for pg_rewind

CREATE ROLE repuser WITH REPLICATION LOGIN PASSWORD '...';

# pg_hba.conf on primary:
host replication repuser <standby-address>/<mask> scram-sha-256

pg_basebackup -D /dba_instance/standby -U repuser -p 5432 -h <primary> -P -R
```

- A `standby.signal` file indicates standby/recovery mode in modern PostgreSQL.
- The standby connects using `primary_conninfo`.
- By default, streaming replication is asynchronous unless synchronous replication is configured.
- Multiple standbys use WAL sender processes on the primary and WAL receiver processes on each standby.

## 12.4 Replication slots

A replication slot retains WAL required by a standby or logical subscriber. This prevents required WAL from being recycled before the consumer receives it.

| Benefit | Risk |
|---|---|
| Standby can reconnect and continue from retained WAL without an immediate new base backup. | A long-down or inactive consumer can cause `pg_wal` to grow until disk space is exhausted. |

```sql
SELECT slot_name, active, restart_lsn
FROM pg_replication_slots;
```

> ⭐ Replication slot = protects required WAL, but a neglected slot can fill the primary's disk.

## 12.5 Common replication lag causes

- Network/receive delay.
- Standby disk I/O bottleneck.
- Standby CPU/replay bottleneck.
- Heavy read workload causing replay conflicts/delay.
- Primary generating WAL faster than standby can receive/replay.
- Replication slot/WAL retention problems.
- Storage capacity or infrastructure problems.

## 12.6 Failover and pg_rewind

After a standby is promoted, the old primary may have diverged onto a different timeline. `pg_rewind` can bring the old primary back in sync with the new primary by copying changed blocks from the divergence point, avoiding a full base backup when prerequisites are met.

> ⭐ `pg_rewind` = rejoin old primary after failover when old and new timelines diverged.

The old server generally needs either data checksums enabled or `wal_log_hints` enabled so changed blocks can be identified.

