# 14. Backup, Restore & PITR

## 14.1 Logical vs physical

| Backup | What it contains | Use |
|---|---|---|
| pg_dump | Logical objects/data for one database. | Migration, selective restore, logical backup. |
| pg_dumpall | Cluster-wide logical SQL, including global objects. | Logical cluster-level dump. |
| pg_basebackup | Physical base backup of a PostgreSQL cluster. | Standby creation, physical restore / PITR foundation. |
| pgBackRest | Physical backup/restore platform with full/diff/incr and WAL archive/PITR. | Production backup / DR. |

> ⭐ PITR needs a physical base backup + continuous WAL archiving/replay. `pg_dump` alone cannot provide physical WAL-based PITR.

## 14.2 PITR flow

```text
PRIMARY
  │
  ├── Base backup (pg_basebackup / pgBackRest)
  │
  └── WAL archive
          │
          ▼
      RESTORE SERVER
          │
          ├── restore base backup
          ├── restore_command
          ├── recovery.signal
          └── recovery_target_time
                    │
                    ▼
                WAL replay
                    │
                    ▼
               target timestamp
                    │
                    ▼
                pg_promote()
```

## 14.3 Practical PITR steps

```text
archive_mode = on
archive_command = 'scp %p osuser@archive-server:/archive/%f'

SELECT pg_switch_wal();

pg_basebackup -p 5432 -h <primary> -D /backup/basebackup -P -c fast

# On restore server:
restore_command = 'cp /archive/%f %p'
recovery_target_time = '2026-09-06 11:59:00'
touch recovery.signal

SELECT pg_is_in_recovery();

# After validating the recovered point:
SELECT pg_promote();
```

- `restore_command`: `%f` is the WAL filename requested; `%p` is the destination path PostgreSQL provides.
- `log_statement = 'ddl'` logs DDL such as `CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DROP`, and `TRUNCATE`; it does not log `INSERT` / `UPDATE` / `DELETE`.
- `pg_wal_replay_resume()` resumes paused replay; it does not itself end recovery. `pg_promote()` promotes a recovered server to normal read-write operation.

## 14.4 Accidental table drop scenario

- Restore the physical base backup to a separate recovery server.
- Configure WAL restore and the recovery target before the destructive event.
- Replay WAL until just before or at the required point.
- Verify with `pg_is_in_recovery()` and validate the target data.
- Export only the required object/data if needed and restore it to production.
- Avoid using PITR directly on production when a separate recovery server can isolate the operation.

